Enforce a 7-byte minimum BR/EDR encryption key size before L2CAP connects (CVE-2019-9506, KNOB).

Applied by kernel/build-bt-modules.sh to its own copy of the tree, never by build-image.sh:
hci_conn.c and l2cap_core.c are in bluetooth.ko, a module, so this changes no byte of the image.

Why: vanilla 4.14.52 reads the negotiated key size (HCI_Read_Encryption_Key_Size, into
hcon->enc_key_size) but never checks it on BR/EDR. KNOB lets a man in the middle push the
negotiation down to a 1-byte key and then brute-force the link, which here carries the HID input
of a pad or keyboard. LE is already protected: smp.c refuses below SMP_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE (7).

Source: the final shape of the mainline series, as 4.14 stable carried it --
  d5bb334a8e17 "Bluetooth: Align minimum encryption key size for LE and BR/EDR connections"
               (HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE 7, matching SMP; the first fix had demanded 16 bytes),
  3ca44c16b0dc "Bluetooth: Fix regression with minimum encryption key size alignment"
               (the check moved out of hci_conn into L2CAP channel setup, because a central
               check in HCI broke links to pre-2.1 / legacy-pairing devices),
  eca94432934f "Bluetooth: Fix faulty expression for minimum encryption key size check"
               (an unencrypted link has no key-size requirement and must pass),
and the hci_conn_security() stall from the same series, so that a link whose key size read is
still in flight answers "not yet" instead of being refused with enc_key_size 0. The
SSP-only gate of 693cd8ce3f88 "Bluetooth: Check key sizes only when Secure Simple Pairing is
enabled" is subsumed by the move into L2CAP and is not a separate hunk.

Changes:
  - include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h: HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE 7.
  - hci_conn_security(): on an encrypted link, return 0 (pending) until enc_key_size is known.
    It is always set when encryption completes -- the read result, or 16 if the controller lacks
    the command or the read fails -- and hci_encrypt_cfm() then re-drives the pending channels.
  - l2cap_check_enc_key_size(): true for an unencrypted link, else enc_key_size >= 7.
  - Called at the four outgoing/accept decision points: l2cap_do_start() and l2cap_security_cfm()
    BT_CONNECT (time the channel out), l2cap_conn_start() (close with ECONNREFUSED),
    l2cap_security_cfm() BT_CONNECT2 (answer L2CAP_CR_SEC_BLOCK).
Not covered, as in 4.14 stable: an incoming L2CAP_CONN_REQ answered directly by l2cap_connect()
on an already-encrypted link is not key-size checked.

--- a/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h
+++ b/include/net/bluetooth/hci_core.h
@@ -178,6 +178,9 @@
 
 #define HCI_MAX_SHORT_NAME_LENGTH	10
 
+/* Min encryption key size to match with SMP */
+#define HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE		7
+
 /* Default LE RPA expiry time, 15 minutes */
 #define HCI_DEFAULT_RPA_TIMEOUT		(15 * 60)
 
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
@@ -1281,8 +1281,16 @@
 		return 0;
 
 encrypt:
-	if (test_bit(HCI_CONN_ENCRYPT, &conn->flags))
+	if (test_bit(HCI_CONN_ENCRYPT, &conn->flags)) {
+		/* Ensure that the encryption key size has been read,
+		 * otherwise stall the upper layer responses.
+		 */
+		if (!conn->enc_key_size)
+			return 0;
+
+		/* Nothing else needed, all requirements are met */
 		return 1;
+	}
 
 	hci_conn_encrypt(conn);
 	return 0;
--- a/net/bluetooth/l2cap_core.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/l2cap_core.c
@@ -1340,6 +1340,21 @@
 		       sizeof(req), &req);
 }
 
+static bool l2cap_check_enc_key_size(struct hci_conn *hcon)
+{
+	/* The minimum encryption key size needs to be enforced by the
+	 * host stack before establishing any L2CAP connections. The
+	 * specification in theory allows a minimum of 1, but to align
+	 * BR/EDR and LE transports, a minimum of 7 is chosen.
+	 *
+	 * This check might also be called for unencrypted connections
+	 * that have no key size requirements. Ensure that the link is
+	 * actually encrypted before enforcing a key size.
+	 */
+	return (!test_bit(HCI_CONN_ENCRYPT, &hcon->flags) ||
+		hcon->enc_key_size >= HCI_MIN_ENC_KEY_SIZE);
+}
+
 static void l2cap_do_start(struct l2cap_chan *chan)
 {
 	struct l2cap_conn *conn = chan->conn;
@@ -1357,9 +1372,14 @@
 	if (!(conn->info_state & L2CAP_INFO_FEAT_MASK_REQ_DONE))
 		return;
 
-	if (l2cap_chan_check_security(chan, true) &&
-	    __l2cap_no_conn_pending(chan))
+	if (!l2cap_chan_check_security(chan, true) ||
+	    !__l2cap_no_conn_pending(chan))
+		return;
+
+	if (l2cap_check_enc_key_size(conn->hcon))
 		l2cap_start_connection(chan);
+	else
+		__set_chan_timer(chan, L2CAP_DISC_TIMEOUT);
 }
 
